Done. Now `gemm_bf16_config_for` refactor to select from the candidates table (§3.11). I'll add `#include "vkernels/kernels/common/heuristics.hpp"` and make each branch return a candidate from the table. The logic stays identical; entries now sourced from the table:

CUDA branch: M<=64: N<=1024 → {16,16,64,32}; else → {16,64,64,128}; else → {64,64,64,256}.
HIP/host: M<=64 → {16,16,64,64}; else → {64,64,64,256}.

I'll rewrite config_for to look up candidates by (bm,bn) — actually cleaner: keep branch logic but assert/return via table entries. To make the connection real (selectors must return measured candidates), I'll select by index from `gemm_bf16_tile_candidates()`. For CUDA: index 0/1/2. For HIP: 0/1. Use a helper that finds the tile by (bm, bn) or just index constants with a static_assert-ish comment. Simpler and honest:

```cpp
void gemm_bf16_config_for(...) {
  const auto candidates = heuristics::gemm_bf16_tile_candidates();
  const heuristics::GemmTile* pick = nullptr;
  *bk = 64;
#if VKERNELS_HAS_CUDA
  (void)K;
  // comments...
  if (M <= 64) {
    pick = &candidates[N <= 1024 ? 0 : 1];
  } else {
    pick = &candidates[2];
  }
#else
  (void)N; (void)K;
  pick = &candidates[M <= 64 ? 0 : 1];
#endif
  *bm = pick->bm; *bn = pick->bn; *threads = pick->threads;
}
```

