Conventions clear. There IS an nvcc (`/usr/local/cuda/bin/nvcc`)! So I can build the CUDA preset and actually run device tests on the GB10 box (torch.cuda works — it was only triton's driver that failed). That means the CUDA kernels I write can be genuinely validated against the oracle on this box! Let me check whether the cuda preset builds currently (the user's WIP CMakeLists may affect). I'll try building preset `cuda` in a fresh build dir later.

Now write the Phase 3 kernels:

**1. mqa_logits** (weighted ReLU MQA scoring, DeepGEMM semantics, dense [M, N] output):

hpp:
```cpp
namespace vkernels::kernels {
// Weighted ReLU MQA logits for the lightning indexer (DeepGEMM semantics).
// q: [M, H, D], kv: [N, D], weights: [M, H], cu_ks/cu_ke: [M].
// out[m, n] = sum_h weights[m, h] * relu(dot(q[m, h, :], kv[n, :]))
//           for n in [cu_ks[m], cu_ke[m]); -inf elsewhere.
void mqa_logits(std::size_t M, std::size_t N, std::size_t H, std::size_t D,
                Span<const float> q, Span<const float> kv, Span<const float> weights,
                Span<const int> cu_seq_len_k_start, Span<const int> cu_seq_len_k_end,
                Span<float> out);
namespace cuda { same signature }
}
```
cpp oracle: straightforward. cu kernel: grid (M, cdiv(N, TILE_N)), each thread computes one (m, n): loop h, d. For scaffold correctness, that's fine (simple but correct); comment notes perf ladder is future work (matching repo philosophy: correctness oracle first).

**2. m_grouped_gemm_nt_masked**:
```cpp
// C[g, m, :] = A[g, m, :] @ B[g].T for m < masked_m[g]; rows >= masked_m[g] are LEFT UNTOUCHED
// (CUDA-graph decode contract: the CPU never knows per-expert token counts).
void m_grouped_gemm_nt_masked(std::size_t G, std::size_t M, std::size_t N, std::size_t K,
                              Span<const float> A, Span<const float> B, Span<const int> masked_m,
                              Span<float> C);
